  <section xml:id="sec-u3-local-linearization-differentiability">
    <title>Local linearization and differentiability</title>

<xi:include href="sec-u3-tangent-planes-linear-approximation.ptx" />

<subsection xml:id="sec-u3-vector-valued-local-linearization">
    <title>Vector-valued local linearization</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="sec-linear-maps-reappear-locally">
      <title>Linear maps reappear locally</title>
      <p>
        A nonlinear map is usually not linear globally. Near one input, however, differentiability replaces small input changes by a linear map.
        <me>F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})\approx F(\mathbf{a})+J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}.</me>
        <m>\mathbf{x}\mapsto F(\mathbf{a})+J_F(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})</m> is affine in <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. <m>\mathbf{h}\mapsto J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}</m> is the linear map on input changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Linear algebra gave us global linear maps <m>\mathbf{x}\mapsto A\mathbf{x}</m>. Calculus gives us local linear maps <m>\mathbf{h}\mapsto J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}</m>. The same matrix language therefore reappears inside nonlinear problems.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <conclusion>
    <definition>
      <title>Linear Approximation of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>F: \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a vector-valued function. Provided that the partial derivatives of the components of <m>F</m> all exist at <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, so that the Jacobian matrix <m>J_F</m> is well-defined, the <term>linear approximation</term> of <m>F</m> at <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is the vector-valued affine function
          <me>
            L(\mathbf{x}) =
            F(\mathbf{a}) + J_F(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a})
          </me>
          where <m>J_F(\mathbf{a}) (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a})</m> is the Jacobian matrix of <m>F</m> evaluated at <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, and then applied to the vector <m>\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a}</m>.
          The approximation is affine in <m>\mathbf{x}</m>; its linear part is the map <m>\mathbf{h} \mapsto J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}</m> on small input changes.
        </p>

        <p>
          The components of <m>L</m> are precisely the linear approximations of the components of <m>F</m>, i.e., <m>L_i(\mathbf{x}) = F_i(\mathbf{a}) + (\nabla F_i)(\mathbf{a})^T(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a})</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <note>
      <p>
        Geometrically, the graph of the linear approximation to a vector-valued function can be visualized as a higher dimensional <q>tangent plane</q> of the graph of the function <m>F</m> at <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>F(x,y) = ( x^2y, xe^y )</m>. Find the linear approximation of <m>F</m> at the point <m>(1,0)</m>, and use it to approximate <m>F(1.1, -0.2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We compute that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              J_F \amp = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ x^2 y \} \amp \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ x^2 y \} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ xe^y \} \amp \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ xe^y \} \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 2xy \amp x^2 \\ e^y \amp xe^y \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          In particular,
          <me>
            J_F(1,0) = \begin{bmatrix} 2(1)(0) \amp (1)^2 \\ e^{(0)} \amp (1)e^{(0)} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
          </me>.
          Since
          <me>
            F(1,0) = \begin{bmatrix} (1)^2(0) \\ (1)e^{(0)} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          </me>.
          So the linear approximation of <m>F</m> at <m>(1,0)</m> is the function
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x - 1 \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1y \\ 1 + (x - 1) + y \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} y \\ x + y \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          So
          <me>
            F(1.1,-0.2) \approx L(1.1,-0.2) = \begin{bmatrix} -0.2 \\ 1.1 - 0.2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -0.2 \\ 0.9 \end{bmatrix}
          </me>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-local-square-grid-test">
      <title>Local square-grid visualization</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to
          <me>
            F\left(\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\end{bmatrix}\right)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              x+y^2\\
              y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The Jacobian matrix is
          <me>
            J_F(x,y)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;2y\\
              0&amp;1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute <m>J_F(\mathbf{a})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Let <m>\mathbf{h}=\begin{bmatrix}h_1\\h_2\end{bmatrix}</m>. Compute <m>F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})-F(\mathbf{a})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the error
              <me>F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})-F(\mathbf{a})-J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>If <m>0 \leq h_2 \leq r</m>, how large can the first coordinate of the error be?</p></li>
          <li><p>What happens to the local square-grid picture as <m>r</m> gets smaller?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>a_2=1/2</m>,
          <me>
            J_F(\mathbf{a})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;1\\
              0&amp;1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          This is the horizontal shear matrix from Unit 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1\\
              1/2+h_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1+(1/2+h_2)^2\\
              1/2+h_2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1+1/4+h_2+h_2^2\\
              1/2+h_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Also,
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{a})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/4\\
              1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})-F(\mathbf{a})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1+h_2+h_2^2\\
              h_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The Jacobian matrix prediction is
          <me>
            J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;1\\
              0&amp;1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1\\
              h_2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_1+h_2\\
              h_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          So the error is
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})-F(\mathbf{a})-J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              h_2^2\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          If <m>0 \leq h_2 \leq r</m>, then <m>0 \leq h_2^2 \leq r^2</m>. As the square around <m>\mathbf{a}</m> shrinks, the nonlinear image and the local linear image become closer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The local linear map acts on the change vector <m>\mathbf{h}</m>, not on the original input vector <m>\mathbf{x}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <note xml:id="note-u3-local-square-grid-test">
      <p>
        The local square-grid visualization does not produce one matrix for the whole nonlinear map. It shows one matrix approximation near one base point.
      </p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u3-local-square-grid-test-large">
      <caption>Local square-grid visualization near <m>\mathbf{a}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1/2\end{bmatrix}</m> for the larger input square <m>r=0.25</m>. The three panels show the input grid, the nonlinear image, and the local linear image on ticked axes.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit3/u3-local-square-grid-test-large" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Local square-grid comparison for the larger input square.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A colored input grid centered at <m>\mathbf{a}=(0,1/2)</m> with radius <m>r=0.25</m> is shown above its nonlinear image and its local linear image. The nonlinear image visibly bends away from the local linear image.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u3-local-square-grid-test-small">
      <caption>Local square-grid visualization near <m>\mathbf{a}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1/2\end{bmatrix}</m> for the smaller input square <m>r=0.1</m>. The nonlinear image is closer to the local linear image than in the larger-square visualization.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit3/u3-local-square-grid-test-small" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Local square-grid comparison for the smaller input square.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A colored input grid centered at <m>\mathbf{a}=(0,1/2)</m> with radius <m>r=0.1</m> is shown above its nonlinear image and its local linear image. The nonlinear and local linear images are closer than for the larger square.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <paragraph xml:id="sec-two-kinds-approximation">
      <title>Two kinds of approximation</title>
      <p>
        The approximation in this unit is local. Near one input <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, a differentiable function behaves like
        <me>F(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})\approx F(\mathbf{a})+J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}.</me>
        Unit 4 studies a different kind of approximation:
        <me>\min_x\|Ax-b\|.</me>
        That is a global best approximation by a vector in a subspace. Unit 3 asks what a nonlinear function looks like near one point; Unit 4 asks which allowable vector is closest overall.
      </p>
    </paragraph>
    </conclusion>
  </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u3-chain-rule-matrix-multiplication">
    <title>Chain rule as matrix multiplication</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Unit 1 used matrix multiplication to represent composition of linear maps: applying <m>B</m> first and then <m>A</m> gives <m>A(B\mathbf{x})=(AB)\mathbf{x}</m>. For differentiable nonlinear maps, the same idea reappears locally. The local linear maps compose, so their Jacobian matrices multiply.
      </p>
    </introduction>

<xi:include href="sec-u3-chain-rule.ptx" />

<subsection xml:id="sec-tiny-nn-forward">
    <title>A tiny sigmoid block</title>
    <p>
      A small neural-network block can be read as a composition of three steps: an affine map, a coordinatewise nonlinear rule, and another affine map.
    </p>

    <note xml:id="optional-u3-relu-locally-linear">
      <title>Sigmoid and its derivative</title>
      <p>
        Let
        <me>
          \sigma(t)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-t}}.
        </me>
        The derivative is
        <me>
          \sigma'(t)=\sigma(t)(1-\sigma(t)).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        When <m>\sigma</m> is applied to a vector, it is applied coordinate by coordinate.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-relu-square-grid-test">
      <title>A tiny sigmoid block</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            W_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0\\
              0&amp;1\\
              1&amp;1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            W_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0&amp;-1/2\\
              0&amp;1&amp;1/2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and let <m>\mathbf{b}_1=\mathbf{0}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}_2=\mathbf{0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define
          <me>
            \mathbf{s}=W_1\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}_1,
            \qquad
            \mathbf{u}=\sigma(\mathbf{s}),
            \qquad
            N(\mathbf{x})=W_2\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{b}_2,
          </me>
          where <m>\sigma</m> is applied coordinatewise.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the base point
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          do the following.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute the pre-activation vector <m>\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}}=W_1\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}_1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the hidden vector <m>\mathbf{u}_{\mathbf{a}}=\sigma(\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>N(\mathbf{a})</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <m>\sigma'(0)=1/4</m> to compute
              <me>
                D_{\mathbf{a}}
                =
                \operatorname{diag}\bigl(\sigma'((\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}})_1),\sigma'((\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}})_2),\sigma'((\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}})_3)\bigr).
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the chain rule to compute
              <me>
                J_N(\mathbf{a})=W_2D_{\mathbf{a}}W_1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use
              <me>
                N(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})\approx N(\mathbf{a})+J_N(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}
              </me>
              to predict the output change caused by a small input change <m>\mathbf{h}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO5, U3-LO7 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>\mathbf{a}=\mathbf{0}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}_1=\mathbf{0}</m>,
          <me>
            \mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}}
            =
            W_1\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_{\mathbf{a}}
            =
            \sigma(\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{a}})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/2\\
              1/2\\
              1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>\mathbf{b}_2=\mathbf{0}</m>,
          <me>
            N(\mathbf{a})
            =
            W_2\mathbf{u}_{\mathbf{a}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0&amp;-1/2\\
              0&amp;1&amp;1/2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/2\\
              1/2\\
              1/2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/4\\
              3/4
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>\sigma'(0)=1/4</m>,
          <me>
            D_{\mathbf{a}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/4&amp;0&amp;0\\
              0&amp;1/4&amp;0\\
              0&amp;0&amp;1/4
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            J_N(\mathbf{a})
            =
            W_2D_{\mathbf{a}}W_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0&amp;-1/2\\
              0&amp;1&amp;1/2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/4&amp;0&amp;0\\
              0&amp;1/4&amp;0\\
              0&amp;0&amp;1/4
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0\\
              0&amp;1\\
              1&amp;1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/8&amp;-1/8\\
              1/8&amp;3/8
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <me>
            N(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})
            \approx
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/4\\
              3/4
            \end{bmatrix}
            +
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/8&amp;-1/8\\
              1/8&amp;3/8
            \end{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{h}.
          </me>
          This gives the intended local prediction: the Jacobian matrix of the composition is a product of local matrices.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <note xml:id="warn-u3-relu-kink-lines">
      <title>Why sigmoid here</title>
      <p>
        The sigmoid function is smooth, so the Jacobian matrix of this block is defined at every input. Some neural-network models use nonsmooth activation rules. Those require extra case analysis. Here the point is the chain rule for Jacobian matrices.
      </p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u3-relu-square-grid-test">
      <caption>Square-grid visualization for a two-output sigmoid block. The map is smooth. The grid bends and compresses because the sigmoid derivative changes with the input. Near one base point, the Jacobian matrix gives the local linear approximation.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit3/u3-sigmoid-square-grid-test" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Square-grid visualization for a two-output sigmoid block.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A colored grid in the unit square is shown next to its smooth image under a two-output sigmoid block. The image bends and compresses because the sigmoid derivatives vary with the input.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-attention-square-grid-test">
      <title>A one-query softmax weighting map</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A single-query attention-style map can be viewed as a nonlinear vector-valued map.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            K=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              3&amp;0\\
              0&amp;3\\
              -3&amp;-3
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            V=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1&amp;0\\
              0&amp;1\\
              0&amp;0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For a query vector
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              q_1\\
              q_2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          define the score vector
          <me>\mathbf{s}=K\mathbf{q},</me>
          the softmax weight vector
          <me>\boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{q})=\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{s}),</me>
          and the output
          <me>F(\mathbf{q})=V^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{q}).</me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\mathbf{s}=K\mathbf{q}</m> in terms of <m>q_1</m> and <m>q_2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>What are the rows of <m>V</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Explain why <m>F(\mathbf{q})</m> is a weighted average of the rows of <m>V</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{0})</m> and <m>F(\mathbf{0})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Explain why <m>F</m> is not a linear map.</p></li>
          <li><p>What operation makes <m>F</m> nonlinear?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO5, U3-LO7, U1-LO8 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{s}
            =
            K\mathbf{q}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              3q_1\\
              3q_2\\
              -3q_1-3q_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The rows of <m>V</m> are the value vectors
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_3=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The softmax vector
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{q})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              \alpha_1(\mathbf{q})\\
              \alpha_2(\mathbf{q})\\
              \alpha_3(\mathbf{q})
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          has positive entries that add to <m>1</m>. Therefore
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{q})
            =
            V^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{q})
            =
            \alpha_1(\mathbf{q})\mathbf{v}_1
            +
            \alpha_2(\mathbf{q})\mathbf{v}_2
            +
            \alpha_3(\mathbf{q})\mathbf{v}_3
          </me>
          is a weighted average of the value vectors.
        </p>
        <p>
          At <m>\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{0}</m>, the score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{s}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0\\
              0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          All three scores are equal, so
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\alpha}(\mathbf{0})
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/3\\
              1/3\\
              1/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            F(\mathbf{0})
            =
            V^T
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/3\\
              1/3\\
              1/3
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1/3\\
              1/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          A linear map must send <m>\mathbf{0}</m> to <m>\mathbf{0}</m>. Since <m>F(\mathbf{0})\ne \mathbf{0}</m>, the map <m>F</m> is not linear.
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix multiplication <m>K\mathbf{q}</m> is linear in <m>\mathbf{q}</m>. The final weighted average is linear in the weights if the weights are already fixed. The nonlinear step is the softmax rule that turns scores into weights.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <note xml:id="note-u3-attention-output-triangle">
      <title>Softmax outputs stay in the value triangle</title>
      <p>
        The output always lies in the triangle spanned by the three value vectors. The query vector does not choose fixed weights. It changes the scores, and the softmax rule changes all weights together.
      </p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u3-attention-square-grid-test">
      <caption>Square-grid visualization for an attention-style map <m>\mathbf{q}\mapsto V^T\operatorname{softmax}(K\mathbf{q})</m>. The output lies in the triangle spanned by the value vectors. The grid bends and compresses because the softmax weights depend nonlinearly on the query.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit3/u3-attention-square-grid-test" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Square-grid visualization for an attention-style softmax map.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A colored square grid of query vectors is shown next to its image under a softmax-weighted average map. The output points lie inside a triangle formed by three value vectors. The transformed grid bends and compresses toward different corners of the triangle.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <note xml:id="ai-u3-transformer-block-nonlinear">
      <title>An attention-style nonlinear block</title>
      <p>
        Unit 1 treated attention as three visible operations:
        <me>
          \text{dot-product scores}\longrightarrow \text{weights}\longrightarrow \text{weighted averages}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        For a sequence of token vectors stored as rows of a matrix <m>X</m>, fixed weight matrices produce
        <me>
          Q=XW_Q,\qquad K=XW_K,\qquad V=XW_V.
        </me>
        The score matrix is
        <me>
          S=QK^T.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The entry <m>S_{ij}</m> compares the query for token <m>i</m> with the key for token <m>j</m>. A weighting rule, such as row-wise softmax, converts each row of <m>S</m> into weights. Write the resulting attention-weight matrix as <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m>. The attention output is
        <me>
          H=A_{\mathrm{att}}V.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> were fixed, then <m>V\mapsto A_{\mathrm{att}}V</m> would be a linear map: each row of <m>H</m> would be a weighted average of rows of <m>V</m>. In attention, <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> is not fixed. It is computed from <m>X</m> through <m>Q</m>, <m>K</m>, scores, and softmax. Thus the full rule <m>X\mapsto H</m> is generally nonlinear.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Unit 3 question is local. Near one fixed input <m>X_0</m>, a nonlinear rule can be approximated by a linear map. After the entries of <m>X</m> and <m>H</m> are arranged as vectors, that local linear map is represented by a Jacobian matrix.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-linear-nonlinear-pieces">
      <title>What depends on the input?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume the weight matrices <m>W_Q</m>, <m>W_K</m>, and <m>W_V</m> are fixed. The input is the token matrix <m>X</m>. Define
          <me>
            Q=XW_Q,\qquad K=XW_K,\qquad V=XW_V,
          </me>
          <me>
            S=QK^T,\qquad
            A_{\mathrm{att}}=\operatorname{softmax}_{\mathrm{rows}}(S),
            \qquad
            H=A_{\mathrm{att}}V.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which objects are fixed before the input <m>X</m> is chosen?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which objects are computed from <m>X</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is <m>X\mapsto XW_Q</m> a linear map when <m>W_Q</m> is fixed?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does <m>S=QK^T</m> depend on <m>X</m> in two places?</p></li>
          <li><p>If <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> were fixed, what would <m>H=A_{\mathrm{att}}V</m> do to the rows of <m>V</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>In actual attention, why is the full rule <m>X\mapsto H</m> not just one linear map?</p></li>
          <li><p>What Unit 3 object describes the local linear approximation near a fixed input <m>X_0</m>?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO5, U3-LO6, U3-LO7 | C+R | Core]</c>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrices <m>W_Q</m>, <m>W_K</m>, and <m>W_V</m> are fixed. The matrices <m>Q</m>, <m>K</m>, <m>V</m>, <m>S</m>, <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m>, and <m>H</m> are computed from <m>X</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rule <m>X\mapsto XW_Q</m> is linear because <m>W_Q</m> is fixed and right multiplication by a fixed matrix distributes over sums and scalar multiples.
        </p>
        <p>
          The score matrix <m>S=QK^T</m> depends on <m>X</m> through <m>Q</m> and through <m>K</m>. The entries of <m>S</m> are dot products between query rows and key rows, and both sets of rows were computed from <m>X</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> were fixed, then <m>H=A_{\mathrm{att}}V</m> would form weighted averages of the rows of <m>V</m>. This is linear in <m>V</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          In attention, <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> is not fixed. The weights are computed from the input through scores and softmax. The values <m>V</m> also depend on <m>X</m>. Therefore the full rule <m>X\mapsto H</m> is generally nonlinear.
        </p>
        <p>
          Near a fixed input <m>X_0</m>, the local linear approximation is represented by a Jacobian matrix after the entries of the input and output matrices are arranged as vectors.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <warning>
      <title>Specify the input</title>
      <p>
        Before asking whether a rule is linear, first say what is variable and what is fixed. The rule <m>V\mapsto A_{\mathrm{att}}V</m> is linear when <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m> is fixed. The attention rule <m>X\mapsto A_{\mathrm{att}}(X)V(X)</m> is generally nonlinear because the weights and values both depend on <m>X</m>.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-comparing-square-grid-tests">
      <title>Comparing square-grid visualizations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          This unit used three square-grid visualizations: the nonlinear shear from <xref ref="act-u3-corners-not-enough"/>, the sigmoid block from <xref ref="act-u3-relu-square-grid-test"/>, and the attention-style softmax map from <xref ref="act-u3-attention-square-grid-test"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Map A:
          <me>
            F\left(\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\end{bmatrix}\right)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
              x+y^2\\
              y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Map B: a two-output sigmoid block
          <me>
            \mathbf{s}=W_1\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}_1,
            \qquad
            \mathbf{u}=\sigma(\mathbf{s}),
            \qquad
            N(\mathbf{x})=W_2\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{b}_2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Map C: an attention-style softmax map
          <me>
            F_{\mathrm{att}}(\mathbf{q})
            =
            V^T\operatorname{softmax}(K\mathbf{q}).
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which map agrees with a linear shear on the four corners but not at an edge midpoint?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which map is built from affine maps with a coordinatewise sigmoid between them?</p></li>
          <li><p>For which map is the local matrix computed by <m>W_2D_{\mathbf{a}}W_1</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which map produces outputs inside the triangle spanned by value vectors?</p></li>
          <li><p>For which map does the nonlinear step come from softmax weights that depend on the input?</p></li>
          <li><p>In all three examples, what does the square-grid visualization show that four corners alone may miss?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO1, U3-LO5, U3-LO7, U1-LO8 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Map A is the nonlinear shear. It can agree with a linear shear on selected points but differ between them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Map B is the sigmoid block. Its Jacobian matrix near a base point has the form <m>W_2D_{\mathbf{a}}W_1</m>, where <m>D_{\mathbf{a}}</m> records the sigmoid derivatives at that base point. The local matrix can change from point to point because the sigmoid derivatives change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Map C is the attention-style softmax map. Its outputs are weighted averages of the rows of <m>V</m>, so they stay inside the triangle spanned by the value vectors. The softmax weights depend on the input.
        </p>
        <p>
          A square-grid visualization can show bending, compression, and changing local behavior that four corners alone may miss.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u3-directional-derivatives">
    <title>Directional derivatives</title>

    <p>
      By <xref ref="def-9-1-linear-approximation"/>, the first-order change in a scalar-valued function near <m>\mathbf a</m> is
      <me>f(\mathbf a+\mathbf h)-f(\mathbf a)\approx\nabla f(\mathbf a)^T\mathbf h.</me>
      To study change along a unit direction <m>\mathbf v</m>, restrict the input to the line
      <me>\mathbf c(t)=\mathbf a+t\mathbf v.</me>
      The resulting one-variable rate of change is the directional derivative.
    </p>

    <p>The chain rule for one-parameter inputs in <xref ref="sec-9-2-chain-rule-1"/> gives the gradient formula below.</p>

    <definition xml:id="def-10-1-directional-derivative">
        <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a scalar-valued function. Then if <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is a unit vector, the <term>directional derivative</term> of <m>f</m> in the direction of the vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, denoted <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f</m>, is defined as
            <me>
            D_{\mathbf{v}} f(\mathbf{a}) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(\mathbf{a} + h \mathbf{v}) - f(\mathbf{a})}{h}
            </me>,
            provided the limit exists.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        The directional derivative represents the rate of change of the function <m>f</m> at the point <m>\mathbf{a}</m> in the direction of the unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>. For a function <m>f(x,y)</m> of two variables, the directional derivative <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f(\mathbf{a})</m> is the slope of the tangent line to the curve obtained by intersecting the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> with the plane containing both the line passing through <m>\mathbf{a}</m> with direction vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, and the <m>z</m> axis.
    </p>

    <remark>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Partial derivatives are a special case of directional derivatives, i.e., we have
            <me>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i} = D_{\mathbf{e}_i} f
            </me>.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </remark>

    <p>
        Despite partial derivatives being a special case of directional derivatives, in most circumstances we can compute directional derivatives using partial derivatives, or equivalently, the gradient.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient">
        <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose <m>f</m> is a function differentiable at a point <m>\mathbf{a}</m>. Then for each unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f(\mathbf{a}) = \nabla f(\mathbf{a}) \cdot \mathbf{v}</m>.
        </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
        <p>
            Define the path <m>\mathbf{c}(t)=\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{v}</m> and the single-variable function <m>g(t)=f(\mathbf{c}(t))=f(\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{v})</m>. Since <m>\mathbf{c}(0)=\mathbf{a}</m>, we have <m>g(0)=f(\mathbf{a})</m>. Therefore, the definition of the directional derivative gives
            <md>
            <mrow>D_{\mathbf{v}}f(\mathbf{a}) \amp= \lim_{h\to 0}\frac{f(\mathbf{a}+h\mathbf{v})-f(\mathbf{a})}{h}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{g(h)-g(0)}{h}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=g'(0).</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Write <m>\mathbf{a}=(a_1,\ldots,a_n)</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}=(v_1,\ldots,v_n)</m>. The <m>i</m>th coordinate of <m>\mathbf{c}(t)</m> is <m>c_i(t)=a_i+tv_i</m>, so <m>c_i'(0)=v_i</m>. Because <m>g=f\circ\mathbf{c}</m>, the chain rule (<xref ref="sec-9-2-chain-rule-1"/>) gives
            <md>
            <mrow>g'(0) \amp= \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(\mathbf{c}(0))c_i'(0)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(\mathbf{a})v_i</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\cdot\mathbf{v}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Combining the two calculations proves that <m>D_{\mathbf{v}}f(\mathbf{a})=\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>.
        </p>
        </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/> greatly simplifies the computations of directional derivatives.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-dot-product-controls-direction">
      <title>Alignment with the gradient</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose
          <me>\nabla f(\mathbf{a})=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          Compute <m>D_{\mathbf{v}}f(\mathbf{a})</m> for each unit vector below:
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_3=\frac15\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_4=-\frac15\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>Which listed direction gives the greatest increase? Which gives the greatest decrease?</p>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO8, U1-LO1 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/>,
          <me>D_{\mathbf v_1}f(\mathbf a)=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}=3,</me>
          and
          <me>D_{\mathbf v_2}f(\mathbf a)=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}=4.</me>
          Also,
          <me>D_{\mathbf v_3}f(\mathbf a)=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}\cdot\frac15\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}=5,</me>
          while
          <me>D_{\mathbf v_4}f(\mathbf a)=-5.</me>
          The greatest listed increase occurs in direction <m>\mathbf v_3</m>, which is aligned with the gradient. The greatest listed decrease occurs in direction <m>\mathbf v_4</m>, which points opposite the gradient.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
        <p>
            Let <m>f(x,y) = x^2 y + y^2</m>.
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
            Compute the directional derivative <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f(1,2)</m>, where <m>\mathbf{v} = (1/\sqrt{2}, 1/\sqrt{2})</m>, and <m>f(x,y) = x^2y + y^2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
            We begin by computing the gradient of <m>f</m> at <m>(1,2)</m>. We have
            <me>
                f_x(x,y) = 2xy \quad\text{and}\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2 + 2y
            </me>.
            So <m>\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy, x^2 + 2y)</m>, and in particular
            <me>\nabla f(1,2) = \begin{bmatrix} 2(1)(2) \\ (1)^2 + 2 (2) \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</me>.
            The given vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is already a unit vector because
            <me>
                \|\mathbf{v}\|=\sqrt{\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2+\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2}=1.
            </me>
            Therefore, we may apply the directional-derivative formula directly:
            <me>
                D_{\mathbf{v}} f(1,2) = \nabla f(1,2) \cdot \mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix} 1/\sqrt{2} \\ 1/\sqrt{2} \end{bmatrix} = 9 / \sqrt{2}
            </me>.
            </p>
        </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
            Find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at the point <m>(-1,3)</m> in the direction of the vector <m>(-1,\sqrt{3})</m>. That is, compute <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f(-1,3)</m>, where <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is the unit vector in the direction of <m>(-1,\sqrt{3})</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
            The vector
            <me>
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -1\\
                \sqrt{3}
                \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            has norm
            <me>
                \sqrt{(-1)^2+(\sqrt{3})^2}=2
            </me>.
            So the unit vector in this direction is
            <me>
                \mathbf{v}
                =
                \frac12
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -1\\
                \sqrt{3}
                \end{bmatrix}
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -1/2\\
                \sqrt{3}/2
                \end{bmatrix}
            </me>.
            For
            <me>
                f(x,y)=x^2y+y^2,
            </me>
            we have
            <me>
                \nabla f(x,y)=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                2xy\\
                x^2+2y
                \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Thus
            <me>
                \nabla f(-1,3)=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -6\\
                7
                \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Therefore
            <me>
                D_{\mathbf{v}}f(-1,3)
                =
                \nabla f(-1,3)\cdot \mathbf{v}
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -6\\
                7
                \end{bmatrix}
                \cdot
                \begin{bmatrix}
                -1/2\\
                \sqrt{3}/2
                \end{bmatrix}
                =
                3+\frac{7\sqrt{3}}{2}
                =
                \frac{6+7\sqrt{3}}{2}
            </me>.
            </p>
        </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>(2,1)</m> in the direction from <m>(2,1)</m> to <m>(3,5)</m>.
        </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
        <p>
            The unit vector pointing in the direction from <m>(2,1)</m> to <m>(3,5)</m> is the unit vector pointing in the same direction as the vector <m>(3-2,5-1) = (1,4)</m>. This vector has magnitude <m>\sqrt{1^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{17}</m>, so the unit vector pointing in this direction is equal to
            <me>
            \mathbf{v} =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/\sqrt{17}\\
            4/\sqrt{17}
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>.
            Now
            <me>
            \nabla f(x,y) =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3x^2 y\\
            x^3
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>,
            and in particular,
            <me>
            \nabla f(2,1) =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3(2)^2 (1)\\
            (2)^3
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            12\\
            8
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>.
            So
            <me>
            D_{\mathbf{v}} f(2,1) = \nabla f(2,1) \cdot \mathbf{v} =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            12\\
            8
            \end{bmatrix}
            \cdot
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/\sqrt{17}\\
            4/\sqrt{17}
            \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{12 + 32}{\sqrt{17}} = \frac{44}{\sqrt{17}}
            </me>.
        </p>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f(x,y)=x^2e^y</m>. Find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>(2,0)</m> in the direction pointing from <m>(2,0)</m> to <m>(3,0)</m>.
        </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
        <p>
            A vector pointing from <m>(2,0)</m> to <m>(3,0)</m> is
            <me>
            \mathbf{d}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3-2\\
            0-0
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Its norm is
            <me>
            \|\mathbf{d}\|=\sqrt{1^2+0^2}=1.
            </me>
            Thus this vector is already a unit vector. Therefore the required direction is
            <me>
            \mathbf{v}=\mathbf{d}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}
            =\mathbf{e}_1.
            </me>
            Next,
            <me>
            \nabla f(x,y)=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2xe^y\\
            x^2e^y
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \nabla f(2,0)=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4\\
            4
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Thus
            <md>
            <mrow>D_{\mathbf{v}}f(2,0) \amp= \nabla f(2,0)\cdot\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\begin{bmatrix}4\\4\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=4\cdot 1+4\cdot 0=4.</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-10-1-directional-derivative-extrema">
        <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose that a function <m>f</m> is differentiable at a point <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.
            <ul>
            <li>
                If <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{a}) = 0</m>, then <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f(\mathbf{a}) = 0</m> for any unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
                If <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\ne \mathbf{0}</m>, then <m>D_{\mathbf{v}}f(\mathbf{a})</m> is maximized, among unit vectors <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, when
                <me>
                \mathbf{v}=\frac{\nabla f(\mathbf{a})}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\|}.
                </me>
                It is minimized when
                <me>
                \mathbf{v}=-\frac{\nabla f(\mathbf{a})}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\|}.
                </me>
                The maximum value is <m>\|\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\|</m>, and the minimum value is <m>-\|\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\|</m>.
            </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf 0</m>, then <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/> gives
          <me>D_{\mathbf v}f(\mathbf a)=0</me>
          for every unit vector <m>\mathbf v</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now suppose <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)\neq\mathbf 0</m>, and let <m>\theta</m> be the angle between <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)</m> and a unit vector <m>\mathbf v</m>. By <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/> and <xref ref="thm-u1-angle-between-vectors"/>,
          <me>D_{\mathbf v}f(\mathbf a)=\nabla f(\mathbf a)\cdot\mathbf v=\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|\cos\theta.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The largest possible value of <m>\cos\theta</m> is <m>1</m>, attained when <m>\mathbf v</m> points in the direction of the gradient. The smallest possible value is <m>-1</m>, attained when <m>\mathbf v</m> points in the opposite direction. Therefore the maximum and minimum directional derivatives are
          <me>\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|\quad\text{and}\quad-\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|,</me>
          attained at
          <me>\mathbf v=\frac{\nabla f(\mathbf a)}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf v=-\frac{\nabla f(\mathbf a)}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|},</me>
          respectively.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u3-gradient-directions">
        <caption>The gradient indicates the maximum and minimum values of the directional derivative at a point. Figure 4.41 from Edwin <q>Jed</q> Herman and Gilbert Strang, <em>Calculus Volume 3</em>, OpenStax, © 2018 Rice University, licensed under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" visual="CC BY-NC-SA 4.0"/>; source: <url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-6-directional-derivatives-and-the-gradient#CNX_Calc_Figure_14_06_007" visual="OpenStax Figure 4.41"/>.</caption>
        <image source="calc3-fig-4-41.png" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
            Gradient directions at a point on a surface.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
            <p>
            A bowl-shaped surface is shown above the <m>xy</m>-plane with a point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> on the side of the surface. On the plane below, arrows based at the corresponding point show <m>\nabla f</m> for most rapid increase, <m>-\nabla f</m> for most rapid decrease, and perpendicular directions for zero change in <m>f</m>. Dashed vertical guide lines connect the point on the surface to its location in the domain.
            </p>
        </description>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <activity>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Find the direction for which the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y) = 3x^2 - 4xy + 2y^2</m> at <m>(-2,3)</m> is a maximum. What is the maximum value of the directional derivative?
        </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
        <p>
            First, we calculate that
            <me>
            \nabla f(x,y) =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6x - 4y\\
            -4x + 4y
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>.
            So
            <me>
            \nabla f(-2,3) =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6(-2) - 4(3)\\
            -4(-2) + 4(3)
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -24\\
            20
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>.
            <xref ref="thm-10-1-directional-derivative-extrema"/> tells us that the directional derivative <m>D_{\mathbf{v}} f</m> is maximized when <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is the unit vector pointing in the direction of <m>(-24,20)</m>. Since <m>(-24,20)</m> has magnitude <m>\sqrt{(-24)^2 + (20)^2} = \sqrt{976}</m>, we see the direction which maximizes the directional derivative is
            <me>
            \mathbf{v} =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -24/\sqrt{976}\\
            20/\sqrt{976}
            \end{bmatrix}
            </me>,
            and the value of the directional derivative in this direction is equal to the magnitude of the gradient, which is <me>\sqrt{976}</me>.
        </p>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
    <statement>
        <p>
        Calculate <m>D_{\mathbf{u}}f(1, -2, 3)</m> in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v} = -\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j} + 2\mathbf{k}</m> for the function
        <me>
            f(x,y,z) = 5x^2 - 2xy + y^2 - 4yz + z^2 + 3xz.
        </me>
        </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>
        We compute that
        <md>
            <mrow>\|\mathbf{v}\| \amp = \sqrt{(-1)^2 + (2)^2 + (2)^2} = \sqrt{9} = 3</mrow>
        </md>
        So the unit vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is the vector
        <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{u} \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{v}\|} = -\frac{1}{3}\mathbf{i} + \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{j} + \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{k}</mrow>
        </md>
        Using the formula for the directional derivative, we find that
        <md>
            <mrow>D_{\mathbf{u}}f(x,y,z) \amp = \nabla f(x,y,z) \cdot \mathbf{u}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \begin{bmatrix}10x - 2y\\ -2x + 2y - 4z\\ -4y + 2z + 3x\end{bmatrix} \cdot \mathbf{u}</mrow>
            <mrow>
            \amp = - \frac{10x - 2y}{3} + 2 \frac{-2x + 2y - 4z}{3} + 2 \frac{-4y + 2z + 3x}{3}
            </mrow>
        </md>.
        At the point <m>(1, -2, 3)</m>, we evaluate that
        <me>
            D_{\mathbf{u}}f(1,-2,3) = (23)\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) + (-18)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) + (17)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) = \frac{-25}{3}
        </me>
        </p>
    </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>In <xref ref="sec-orthogonal-complements-and-projections"/>, the same dot product will be interpreted as a signed projection coefficient of the gradient onto the chosen direction.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u3-critical-points-gradient-descent">
    <title>Critical points and gradient descent</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Directional derivatives describe first-order change along a chosen direction. We now use them to identify candidates for local extrema and to motivate an iterative method for decreasing a function.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <xi:include href="sec-u5-optimization-critical-points.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="sec-u5-directional-derivatives-gradient-descent.ptx" />
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u3-applications-computation-recap">
    <title>Applications and computation recap</title>

  <table>
    <title>Reading Unit 3 computations</title>
    <tabular>
      <col width="27%"/>
      <col width="33%"/>
      <col width="40%"/>
      <row header="yes"><cell><p>Code fragment</p></cell><cell><p>First question to ask</p></cell><cell><p>Typical interpretation</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><p><m>J_F(\mathbf a)</m></p></cell><cell><p>What are the input and output dimensions?</p></cell><cell><p>Jacobian matrix representing the local linear map.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><p><m>J_F(\mathbf a)\mathbf h</m></p></cell><cell><p>What small input change does <m>\mathbf h</m> represent?</p></cell><cell><p>Predicted first-order output change.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><p><m>J_F(G(\mathbf a))J_G(\mathbf a)</m></p></cell><cell><p>Do the inner dimensions match?</p></cell><cell><p>Jacobian matrix of a composition; the right factor acts first.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><c>grad_f(x)</c></cell><cell><p>What function is being minimized?</p></cell><cell><p>Gradient at the current point.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><c>x - alpha * grad_f(x)</c></cell><cell><p>Is the sign negative?</p></cell><cell><p>One gradient descent step.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><c>losses.append(f(x))</c></cell><cell><p>What is being recorded?</p></cell><cell><p>Loss history during an iteration.</p></cell></row>
      <row><cell><c>np.outer(g, h)</c></cell><cell><p>What are the shapes of <c>g</c> and <c>h</c>?</p></cell><cell><p>Final-layer outer-product gradient.</p></cell></row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-u3-review-checkpoints">
    <title>Review checkpoints</title>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-recap-symbolic-jacobian">
      <title>Reading a symbolic Jacobian matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
F = sp.Matrix([x*y, x**2 + y])
J = F.jacobian([x, y])
J
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <me>
          J=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          y\amp x\\
          2x\amp 1
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What are the component functions of <c>F</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is the shape of <c>J</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does the first column of <c>J</c> measure?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The component functions are <m>F_1(x,y)=xy</m> and <m>F_2(x,y)=x^2+y</m>. The Jacobian matrix is
          <m>\begin{bmatrix}y&amp;x\\2x&amp;1\end{bmatrix}</m>, so it is <m>2\times 2</m>. Its first column measures how the two output components change with respect to the first input variable <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-recap-actual-vs-linear">
      <title>Actual output versus local prediction</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[def F(v):
    x, y = v
    return np.array([x**2 + y, x - y**2])

a = np.array([1.0, 2.0])
h = np.array([0.1, -0.2])
J_at_a = np.array([[2.0, 1.0],
                   [1.0, -4.0]])

actual = F(a + h)
linear = F(a) + J_at_a @ h
actual, linear, actual - linear
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\texttt{actual}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}3.01\\-2.14\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>\texttt{linear}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}3.00\\-2.10\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>\texttt{actual}-\texttt{linear}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.01\\-0.04\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
        </md>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which output is the true nonlinear value?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which output is the local linear prediction?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does <c>actual - linear</c> measure?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The true value is <c>actual</c>, which is <c>[3.01, -2.14]</c>. The local linear prediction is <c>linear</c>, which is <c>[3.0, -2.1]</c>. The difference <c>actual - linear</c> is <c>[0.01, -0.04]</c>, the local approximation error for this input change.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-recap-jacobian-columns">
      <title>Reading Jacobian matrix columns</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[J_at_a = np.array([[2.0, -1.0, 0.0],
                   [0.0,  3.0, 4.0]])

e1_change = np.array([0.1, 0.0, 0.0])
J_at_a[:, 0], J_at_a @ e1_change
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\texttt{J\_at\_a[:, 0]}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}2.0\\0.0\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>\texttt{J\_at\_a @ e1\_change}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.2\\0.0\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
        </md>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What are the input and output dimensions?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does <c>J_at_a[:, 0]</c> select?</p></li>
          <li><p>What output change is predicted for <c>e1_change</c>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The input dimension is <m>3</m> and the output dimension is <m>2</m>. The slice <c>J_at_a[:, 0]</c> selects the first column of the Jacobian matrix, <c>[2.0, 0.0]</c>. For the input change <c>[0.1, 0.0, 0.0]</c>, the predicted output change is <c>[0.2, 0.0]</c>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-recap-chain-rule-shapes">
      <title>Chain-rule shape check</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Jg = np.ones((3, 2))
Jf_at_g = np.ones((4, 3))

Jf_at_g @ Jg
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <me>
          \texttt{Jf\_at\_g @ Jg}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          3.0\amp3.0\\
          3.0\amp3.0\\
          3.0\amp3.0\\
          3.0\amp3.0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which map is applied first in the composition?</p></li>
          <li><p>What are the input and output dimensions of the composition?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is the shape of the product?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The map whose Jacobian matrix is <c>Jg</c> is applied first. Since <c>Jg</c> is <m>3\times 2</m>, it takes two input directions to three intermediate directions; <c>Jf_at_g</c> then takes those three directions to four output directions. The product has shape <m>4\times 2</m>, and the displayed output is a <m>4\times 2</m> array whose entries are all <m>3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-recap-nonlinear-block">
      <title>Reading a tiny sigmoid block</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[def sigmoid(t):
    return 1 / (1 + np.exp(-t))

W1 = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 1.0],
])

W2 = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0, -0.5],
    [0.0, 1.0,  0.5],
])

b1 = np.zeros(3)
b2 = np.zeros(2)

x = np.array([0.0, 0.0])

s = W1 @ x + b1
u = sigmoid(s)
D = np.diag(u * (1 - u))
y = W2 @ u + b2
J_at_x = W2 @ D @ W1

s, u, y, J_at_x
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathbf s\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.0\\0.0\\0.0\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>\mathbf u\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.5\\0.5\\0.5\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>\mathbf y\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.25\\0.75\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
          <mrow>J_{\mathbf x}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0.125\amp-0.125\\0.125\amp0.375\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
        </md>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which lines contain affine maps?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which line contains the coordinatewise nonlinear step?</p></li>
          <li><p>What do <c>s</c>, <c>u</c>, and <c>y</c> represent?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which line forms the diagonal derivative matrix?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which line computes the Jacobian matrix of the block at <c>x</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is the shape of <c>J_at_x</c>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The lines <c>s = W1 @ x + b1</c> and <c>y = W2 @ u + b2</c> are affine maps. The line <c>u = sigmoid(s)</c> is the coordinatewise nonlinear step.
        </p>
        <p>
          The vector <c>s</c> is the pre-activation vector. The vector <c>u</c> is the hidden vector after applying sigmoid. The vector <c>y</c> is the output vector.
        </p>
        <p>
          The line <c>D = np.diag(u * (1 - u))</c> forms the diagonal matrix of sigmoid derivatives at this input, since <m>\sigma'(t)=\sigma(t)(1-\sigma(t))</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The line <c>J_at_x = W2 @ D @ W1</c> computes the Jacobian matrix of the block at <c>x</c> by the chain rule for Jacobian matrices. The shape of <c>J_at_x</c> is <m>2\times 2</m>: the input has two coordinates and the output has two coordinates.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="sec-u5-code-gradient-loop">
      <title>Reading a gradient descent loop</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume <c>grad_f(x)</c> computes <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{x})</m>. Consider the code:
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[x = x0
for k in range(num_steps):
    x = x - alpha * grad_f(x)
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What mathematical update rule is represented?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which quantity is the learning rate?</p></li>
          <li><p>What shape must <c>grad_f(x)</c> have?</p></li>
          <li><p>What would change if the minus sign were a plus sign?</p></li>
          <li><p>Does this code prove that a minimum has been found?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO8, U3-LO9 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The loop represents the update
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}_{k+1}=\mathbf{x}_k-\alpha\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_k).
          </me>
          The learning rate is <c>alpha</c>. The vector <c>grad_f(x)</c> must have the same shape as <c>x</c>, since the update subtracts one vector from another. If the minus sign were replaced by a plus sign, the update would move in the direction of steepest increase instead of steepest decrease. The loop does not prove that a minimum has been found. It only describes the repeated update; convergence depends on the function, starting point, learning rate, and stopping rule.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <title>Diagnosing learning rates</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, start at <m>x_0=4</m> and use the update
          <me>
            x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha f'(x_k).
          </me>
          The table shows the loss values <m>f(x_k)</m> for three learning rates.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="30%"/>
          <col width="30%"/>
          <col width="30%"/>
          <row header="yes"><cell><p><m>k</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\alpha = 0.05</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\alpha = 0.20</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\alpha = 1.05</m></p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p>0</p></cell><cell><p>16.000</p></cell><cell><p>16.000</p></cell><cell><p>16.000</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>12.960</p></cell><cell><p>5.760</p></cell><cell><p>19.360</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p>2</p></cell><cell><p>10.498</p></cell><cell><p>2.074</p></cell><cell><p>23.426</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p>3</p></cell><cell><p>8.503</p></cell><cell><p>0.746</p></cell><cell><p>28.345</p></cell></row>
        </tabular>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which learning rate is making slow but steady progress?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which learning rate is making faster useful progress?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which learning rate appears unstable?</p></li>
          <li><p>Does a decreasing loss table prove that the global minimum has been found?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO9 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The learning rate <m>\alpha=0.05</m> is making slow but steady progress. The learning rate <m>\alpha=0.20</m> is making faster useful progress. The learning rate <m>\alpha=1.05</m> appears unstable because the loss is increasing. A decreasing loss table does not prove that the global minimum has been found. It only shows what happened for the displayed iterates.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <p>
      <em>Interpretation check.</em> Given a Jacobian matrix <m>J_F(\mathbf{a})</m>, identify the input dimension, output dimension, and what each column measures. <em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO3, U3-LO5 | C+R+T | Core]</c>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Exam-style check.</em> Given a <m>3\times 2</m> Jacobian matrix <m>J_F(\mathbf{a})</m>, identify the input dimension, output dimension, and compute the local change <m>J_F(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}</m> for a given small vector <m>\mathbf{h}</m>. <em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO3, U3-LO5 | P+C+R | Core]</c>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Exam skill.</em> Interpret a Jacobian matrix as a local linear map and use it to predict a small output change. <em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO5 | C+R | Core]</c>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-u3-training-objectives-final-layer-updates">
    <title>Training objectives and final-layer updates</title>
    <p>
      Squared-error fitting with fixed features is a least-squares problem. Other training losses are not. The following activities read two general gradient-descent calculations; neither is a consequence of least squares.
    </p>

    <p xml:id="ai-u5-next-token-optimization">
      A language model produces scores for possible next tokens, converts those scores into probabilities, and uses a loss to measure the probability assigned to the observed next token. A simplified objective for a sequence <m>t_1,\ldots,t_L</m> is
      <me>
        \mathcal L(\boldsymbol{\theta})
        =
        -\sum_{i=1}^{L-1}
        \log p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}
        \bigl(t_{i+1}\mid t_1,\ldots,t_i\bigr).
      </me>
      The parameter vector <m>\boldsymbol{\theta}</m> may collect many matrices and bias vectors. This is a general nonlinear optimization problem, not a linear least-squares problem.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-reading-next-token-loss-optimization">
      <title>Reading a next-token loss</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A simplified language-model training objective can be written
          <me>
            \mathcal L(\boldsymbol{\theta})
            =
            -\sum_{i=1}^{L-1}
            \log p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}
            \bigl(t_{i+1}\mid t_1,\ldots,t_i\bigr).
          </me>
          A gradient descent step has the form
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}
            =
            \boldsymbol{\theta}_k
            -\alpha\nabla\mathcal L(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k).
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What are the parameters?</p></li>
          <li><p>What quantity is being minimized?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which symbol is the learning rate?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does the gradient point toward?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is this an optimization problem, even though we are not studying full model training?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is this not automatically a least-squares problem?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO9 | C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The parameters are collected in <m>\boldsymbol{\theta}</m>. The objective <m>\mathcal L(\boldsymbol{\theta})</m> is the loss. The learning rate is <m>\alpha</m>. The gradient <m>\nabla\mathcal L(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k)</m> gives the local direction of steepest increase, so the negative gradient is used for descent. This is an optimization problem because training means adjusting parameters to reduce a loss.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing in the displayed objective has the form
          <me>\|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2</me>
          with a fixed matrix <m>A</m> and a single trained coefficient vector <m>\mathbf c</m>. It is a general differentiable loss optimized by gradient descent.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-final-layer-gradient">
      <title>Gradient of a final linear layer</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a fixed hidden representation is
          <me>\mathbf h\in\mathbb R^d,</me>
          and a final linear layer computes
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\ell}=W\mathbf h,
            \qquad
            W\in\mathbb R^{m\times d}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf g=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}\mathcal L
            \in\mathbb R^m
          </me>
          be the loss gradient with respect to the output scores.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For
              <me>\ell_i=\sum_{j=1}^d W_{ij}h_j,</me>
              use the chain rule to show that
              <me>
                \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial W_{ij}}
                =
                g_i h_j.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Conclude that
              <me>\nabla_W\mathcal L=\mathbf g\mathbf h^T.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>What is the shape of this matrix?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does it have rank at most one?</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Interpret the update
              <me>
                W_{\mathrm{new}}
                =
                W-\alpha\mathbf g\mathbf h^T.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO6, U3-LO9, U2-LO3 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since
          <me>\ell_k=\sum_{j=1}^d W_{kj}h_j,</me>
          we have
          <me>
            \frac{\partial \ell_k}{\partial W_{ij}}
            =
            \begin{cases}
            h_j,&amp;k=i,\\
            0,&amp;k\neq i.
            \end{cases}
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The chain rule gives
          <me>
            \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial W_{ij}}
            =
            \sum_{k=1}^m
            \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\ell_k}
            \frac{\partial\ell_k}{\partial W_{ij}}
            =
            g_i h_j.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>\nabla_W\mathcal L=\mathbf g\mathbf h^T.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>\mathbf g\in\mathbb R^m</m> and <m>\mathbf h\in\mathbb R^d</m>, then this outer product has shape <m>m\times d</m>. Every column is a scalar multiple of <m>\mathbf g</m>, so its rank is at most one. The update changes <m>W</m> in the negative-gradient direction for this training example.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
      <title>The same final-layer update in code</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[G = np.outer(g, h)
W_new = W - alpha * G
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which earlier activity gives the formula represented by <c>G</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is the shape of <c>G</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is <c>np.outer(g, h)</c> used here?</p></li>
          <li><p>What mathematical update is represented by the second line?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is this connected to rank?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U3-LO6, U3-LO9, U2-LO3 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="act-u5-final-layer-gradient" text="custom">Gradient of a final linear layer</xref>,
          <me>G=\mathbf g\mathbf h^T=\nabla_W\mathcal L.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>\mathbf g</m> has length <m>m</m> and <m>\mathbf h</m> has length <m>d</m>, then <c>G</c> has shape <m>m\times d</m>. The command <c>np.outer(g, h)</c> forms the outer product even when the two vectors are stored as one-dimensional NumPy arrays. The second line represents
          <me>
            W_{\mathrm{new}}
            =
            W-\alpha\nabla_W\mathcal L.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The gradient matrix has rank at most one for this single training example. This does not imply that <m>W</m> or <m>W_{\mathrm{new}}</m> has rank one.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="lab-u3-callout">
    <title>Linked notebook</title>
    <p>
      Run <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u3">Lab U3: Jacobian matrices and local linearization</url>. The core path practices symbolic Jacobian matrices, evaluating a Jacobian matrix at a point, local linear prediction, square-grid visualizations, checking Jacobian matrix shapes in the chain rule, and short nonlinear model-block computations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Directional derivatives and gradient descent are revisited computationally at the beginning of <xref ref="lab-u5-callout" text="custom">Lab U5</xref>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a quick reference on array shapes, matrix products, numerical checks, and SymPy symbolic commands, see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-arrays-shapes" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numerical-checks" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-sympy" text="global"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  </section>

